  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how a proton–proton collision can create a strange quark and an antistrange quark, and how the spins of the particles they later form may remain linked or become independent depending on distance. At the top, the collision produces an \\(s\\) and \\(\\bar{s}\\) pair with indicated spin directions. These then hadronize into a Lambda hyperon and an Antilambda hyperon, each shown as a three-quark bound state containing the strange or antistrange quark.\n\nThe main idea is a comparison between two regimes. When the Lambda and Antilambda are produced close together, the spins of the strange and antistrange quarks appear to stay correlated, so the spins of the resulting hyperons are aligned in a related way. This is labeled as spin correlation over small separations. In contrast, when the hyperons are separated by a larger distance, the diagram shows their spin directions becoming uncorrelated, implying that the original quark spin connection is lost or no longer visible after hadronization.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a mechanism linking quark-pair creation in high-energy collisions to observable spin correlations in hyperon–antihyperon pairs, with separation distance as the key factor.",
